Let a frontend with no window lay a small map window out at the size it shows it

T2DMap's outlines, line weights and the room size below which a room's border fades (12 pixels) and then goes (4)
are in pixels. An app that draws a small mapper window onto a much larger part of its screen, as a page of the
starter UI's dock is on a phone, so lays its rooms out tiny and magnifies them: the levels above and below become
filled dots, the rooms lose their borders and the exits grow thin, unlike the app's own map of the same area.

- THeadlessSession::renderMap() takes a layoutScale, from 1 to 16 and 1 by default: the map is laid out at the size
  times that, its rooms as large in those units as they are shown, and drawn scaled down to the size, so it looks as
  a map laid out at that larger size does. Cosmetic pens stay their width in device pixels either way.
- TNullMapView keeps the size and scale of the last render(): mapPointer(), moveMapView(), mapView() and the rooms it
  lists stay in the size's pixels, and are carried to the layout's for TMapPainter.
- The zoom, the rooms across the narrow side, is the area's as before.
- docs/headless.md describes it.

Tested by Mudlet/android's native/tests/test_mudlet_engine.py (map_layout: a 100 by 100 mapper shown four times as
large drawn as the app's 400 by 400 map of the same rooms, its view and a press on a room still in the window's
pixels, and laid out in its own pixels again without it). The desktop is unchanged.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.
Upstream: not opened yet

